  "enhanced_information": "In the case of an 83-year-old woman with a history of atrial fibrillation and multiple ischemic strokes, brain histology following a stroke would typically show cellular debris due to necrosis and an inflammatory response. Initially, neutrophils would be present, followed by lymphocytes as the inflammatory response evolves. Therefore, the most likely findings on brain histology would include cellular debris and lymphocytes, as well as cellular debris and neutrophils at different stages of the inflammatory process. Cystic cavitation can occur but is not definitive in all cases. Fat saponification is not a typical finding in ischemic stroke, and increased binding of acidophilic dyes indicates necrotic tissue but is not specific to this case. Thus, the best-supported option is 'opa. Cellular debris and lymphocytes.'"
